<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:controls="using:ScreenShare.App.Controls">

  <!--
    The wait, as one rule for every control that can be waiting. A press that crosses to the
    backend takes a round trip, and a control that answers a press by going quietly inert is
    read as a broken one - the uplink measurement taught that, and it used to be the only
    control on the surface that said so.

    Stated once and never at a call site. A screen sets controls:Pending.IsActive from the
    command's own in-flight field and gets this; a screen that drew its own spinner beside its
    own label would be a second answer to the same question, and two answers drift.
  -->

  <!--
    The label goes and the spinner takes its place, inside the same box: the hidden content
    is still measured, so a button does not resize under the pointer the moment it is pressed
    and a row of them does not reflow around the one that is working.

    It is the ContentTemplate that moves rather than the Content, so nothing here writes what
    the control says. The label comes back by itself when the call answers, which is the
    render function's usual property applied to a template.
  -->
  <!--
    A control that is waiting is not a control that is off, so the pointer says the press has
    landed and is being answered rather than being unavailable. The command refuses a second
    press itself, which is what makes the cursor cosmetic rather than load-bearing.
  -->
  <Style Selector=":is(Button):pending">
    <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Wait" />
    <Setter Property="ContentTemplate">
      <DataTemplate>
        <Panel>
          <ContentPresenter Content="{Binding}" Opacity="0" IsHitTestVisible="False" />
          <controls:Spinner />
        </Panel>
      </DataTemplate>
    </Setter>
  </Style>

</Styles>
